[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should a due reminder reach the user? → In-page only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last scoping question before I present a design. Then tooling, then the design itself.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the notifications system needs tasks to observe, and tasks don't exist. How much of that comes with this
  work.

  - [1mTwo slices, tasks first[0m — spec and build a minimal task app (add/edit/complete/delete, due date, localStorage), then
    spec notifications on top of it. Each slice gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle. Slower to first
    notification, but the notification design gets written against a real model instead of a predicted one.
  - [1mOne spec, tasks included as substrate[0m — build the minimal task model and the reminder system together, in one
    design. Faster, and the two are genuinely coupled through the due-date field. Risk: the spec carries two subjects
    and the task-model decisions get less scrutiny than they'd get alone.
  - [1mNotifications against a stub[0m — define a task interface, build reminders against a fake in-memory list, wire it to
    real tasks later. Fastest to a working reminder engine; the integration is where the surprises land.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m one spec, tasks included — but written with the task model as an explicitly minimal substrate
  rather than a co-equal feature. The app is one static page; a full decomposition into two spec/plan cycles is more
  process than 300 lines of client-side code warrants. If the task model turns out to have real design depth once we're
  in it, that's hidden complexity and I'll stop and split it.
